# Line endings are normalized here rather than left to each contributor's
# core.autocrlf setting. The repository already stores every text file with LF;
# this makes that explicit and, more importantly, makes the *working copy*
# consistent too.
#
# That second part matters: Tools/toc-validator.sh silently reported 13 false
# "MISSING" entries on Windows checkouts because MythicPlusTracker.toc had CRLF
# there and LF in CI. Tools/registration-validator.sh had the mirror-image bug,
# passing on Windows and failing on Linux. Platform-dependent line endings are
# how both stayed hidden.
#
# text=auto still lets git detect binary files on its own; eol=lf then applies
# to whatever it classified as text, in the working copy as well as the index.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts are the case where a stray CR actually breaks execution: CI runs
# them with bash, where a trailing CR becomes part of the last token on a line.
# Called out separately so the reason is on record, not because * misses them.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Never normalize binary assets, even if git's heuristic ever misjudges one.
*.png binary

# Same for JPEGs — git's heuristic already classifies them as binary, this only
# makes it explicit next to *.png so no future normalization rule catches them.
*.jpg binary

# The README screenshots belong on GitHub, not in anyone's AddOns folder. The
# generated source archives honour export-ignore, so the release download stays
# lean instead of shipping ~1.8 MB of images users never load.
docs/** export-ignore

# Repo infrastructure that has no business in anyone's AddOns folder. The release
# workflow builds the installable zip with `git archive`
# (.github/workflows/release.yml), so these rules decide what users actually
# unpack — and there, CI configuration, agent settings and the validator scripts
# are pure ballast.
#
# CI itself is unaffected: workflows run on the checkout, not on the archive, so
# Tools/*.sh and .luacheckrc stay available to lua-validation.yml. Contributors
# clone the repo anyway.
#
# .claude/ needs this rule specifically: .claude/settings.json is tracked — it
# was committed before the .gitignore entry — and .gitignore no longer applies to
# tracked files, so export-ignore is the only lever left for the archive.
#
# README, LICENSE, CHANGELOG and the developer docs stay in deliberately: whoever
# opens the zip should be able to read what this is and how to contribute. This
# file and .gitignore do not — pure git metadata that says nothing to anyone in
# an AddOns folder.

# Directory form rather than X/**: with the glob, git archive drops every file
# but still writes an empty directory entry for these three paths, because the
# directory path itself does not match X/**. The docs/** rule above only gets
# away with it because docs/ holds nothing but the screenshots/ subdirectory —
# not something to rely on.

.github/ export-ignore
.claude/ export-ignore
Tools/ export-ignore
.luacheckrc export-ignore
.gitattributes export-ignore
.gitignore export-ignore
